{#
   The public shell, once.

   Until S46 there was no shared layout on the public side at all: **0 of 45
   public templates extended anything**, so all 45 re-declared their own `<head>`,
   their own stylesheet set, their own favicon and their own body scaffolding.
   That is the same disease S29 cured in the admin, on the side members actually
   use, and it is why the public pages drift apart.

   It is deliberately a *separate* base from `base.html.twig`: that one is extended
   by 23 admin templates, and widening it to serve both would have meant changing
   the admin's shell in a session about the public site. S53 can merge them once
   both sides look the same; doing it now would be debugging two changes at once.

   Blocks, in the order a page usually wants them:

     title        the whole <title>, suffix included — pages already carry
                  " - FabOS" and appending it here would have doubled it
     head         anything else that must sit in <head> *before* the stylesheets:
                  `profil`'s theme script, which has to run before first paint,
                  and the `noindex` meta on the two pages reached from a mail
     stylesheets  page-specific sheets — style.css is always loaded first
     page_styles  the page's own <style> block
     body         everything between the header and the footer
     javascripts  the page's scripts. ⚠️ Nothing is loaded by default:
                  main.js is on 29 public pages and absent from the rest, so
                  adding it here would put a script on pages that never had one

   For a class on <body>, `{% set body_class = '…' %}` at the top of the child.
   A variable rather than a block so that a page without one still renders a bare
   `<body>` — a block would leave `class=""` on forty pages that never had it.

   `html_attrs` is the same idea one tag up, and it exists for exactly one page:
   `profil` server-renders `data-theme-preference` on `<html>` so the member's
   chosen theme is on the document before the page's own script runs. Without the
   hook the migration dropped that attribute silently — which is precisely what
   the before/after diff was for.

   ⚠️ `html_attrs` is printed raw, because it is markup. **Escape every value you
   interpolate into it at the call site** — `… ~ user.theme|e('html_attr') ~ …`.
   It is a one-page hook, not a general-purpose attribute bag.

   ⚠️ **The header and footer are inside blocks, not hardcoded**, so a page that
   genuinely needs neither can say so without abandoning the layout. Nothing does
   today; the door is open so the next such page does not copy this file again.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ app.request.locale }}"{% if html_attrs is defined and html_attrs %} {{ html_attrs|raw }}{% endif %}>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{% block title %}FabOS{% endblock %}</title>
    {%- block head %}{% endblock %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('css/style.css') ~ '?v=20260901-s158' }}">
    {# The S51 component set. Loaded for every public page rather than per page,
       because a component that has to be opted into is a component the next page
       reinvents. It is token-only and adds no literal colours. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('css/components.css') ~ '?v=20260901-s158' }}">
    {%- block stylesheets %}{% endblock %}
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ asset('images/favicon.png') }}">
    {%- block page_styles %}{% endblock %}
    {# AssetMapper — Stimulus seul, Turbo délibérément désactivé (assets/controllers.json).

       ⚠️ Sans Turbo, ceci ne change rien à la navigation ni aux scripts existants :
       `main.js` et les blocs inline continuent de s'exécuter exactement comme avant.
       C'est précisément pourquoi Turbo est resté éteint — il aurait cassé les blocs
       accrochés à `DOMContentLoaded` et fait s'empiler ceux qui écoutent `document`.

       ⚠️ Chargé ici pour tout le monde plutôt que par page, pour la même raison que
       components.css juste au-dessus : un composant qu'il faut activer page par page
       est un composant que la page suivante réinvente. #}
    {{ importmap('app') }}
</head>
<body{% if body_class is defined and body_class %} class="{{ body_class }}"{% endif %}>
    {# ⚠️ **S147, J-14 — the skip link is the FIRST tabbable thing on the page.**
       Without it a keyboard user retraversed ten header controls — brand, five
       nav entries, search, its button, the account link, sign in — before
       reaching the content, on every single navigation. It is visually hidden
       until focused (`.skip-link` in `style.css`, shown in `/admin/design`), and
       every `<main>` in this codebase carries `id="main-content"` so it always
       has somewhere to land. #}
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">{{ 'common.skip_to_content'|trans }}</a>
    {% block header %}{% include 'site/_header.html.twig' %}{% endblock %}
    {%- block body %}{% endblock %}
    {% block footer %}{% include 'site/_footer.html.twig' %}{% endblock %}
    {#- ⚠️ `main.js` is emitted BY DEFAULT since S81, where `base.html.twig` has
        always done so. The old note here said adding it would "put a script on
        pages that never had one" — true when it was written, and wrong once
        `_header.html.twig` became universal: that header carries the burger,
        the burger is wired up by `initMobileNavToggle()` in main.js, and **21
        of the 38 pages on this shell never loaded it**. Their menu button did
        nothing at all. A page that wants extra scripts uses `{{ parent() }}`. -#}
    {%- block javascripts %}<script src="{{ asset('js/main.js') ~ '?v=20260811-s134c-i18n' }}"></script>{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
